        <section id="what-are-security-updates?-what-is-patching?"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>What are security updates? What is patching?</h2>
              <p>Let's have a look at security updates and patching in more detail.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Security updates
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>As cyber criminals develop new ways of getting into systems then the security of those systems needs to be updated. This is usually done by regular security updates to a system. While this may seem annoying to have to do, they are very important to keep on top of the latest threats that are out there.</p>
<p>Security updates are patches released by the vendors of that hardware, firmware, OS, or software which address the known CVE vulnerabilities. Security updates should be set to automatically update where possible.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p3_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          What is 'patching'?
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Patching is a process used to make changes to a computer program or its supporting data designed to update, fix, or improve it. This includes fixing security vulnerabilities and other bugs. Vulnerabilities that are most exploited are usually old ones that have the highest impact because they allow remote code execution (RCE) which allows a criminal full access to the device. Patches exist for these and have for many years, but it is often very difficult for a business to patch certain systems. When a system is being patched it may cause downtime or unknown compatibility issues.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p3_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="keeping-up-to-date"  class="t-bg-primary1a">
          
            <h2>Keeping up to date</h2>
              <p>So why is it important to keep cyber security information and maintenance records accurate and up to date?</p>
<p>Let's have a look at what benefits this has.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Accuracy
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Keeping accurate and up to date information and maintenance records on cyber security is essential in helping to understand how systems are being used and collecting logs and good logging practices are fundamental in protective monitoring. They can also be used if a breach happens to evaluate its impact.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p3_accordian1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Breaches
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Being able to retroactively go back through audit logs of what a system and its users were doing is incredibly important especially during a breach. This helps to gain understanding on how far an attacker has managed to penetrate a system and subsequently contain and eradicate the attack.
If the breach is substantial and a regulator such as the ICO becomes involved, it’s important to be able to demonstrate a clear understanding of what has happened and it’s impact so as to reduce any penalties that may be issued following a breach.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p3_accordian2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
